{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the complete MCP planning note, primary91e3d26a57f84b229b09f1fb4e38068415c18d40 at exactb5add513f4a6a00c6a4772ef9bcb6f4a6cd6680a, notes/2026-10-01-mcp-plan.md, under489a992e/932ce20e and queued review711ded30. I read all32,581bytes and582 content lines, SHA256c79336c71296da583a929b497721ddf97380988373b4e8851287f1fcbd004962. The layered direction is useful, but it needs a bounded dated reconciliation before the original planning outcome closes.\n\nPreserve775acdd3's adopted intent: core/toolsets/descriptors and required act idempotency with bounded waits; the additional reads, OAuth, application packs alongside application servers, and the full later capabilities. Estimates and contest dates do not cap functionality. These corrections belong to the existing planning promise and implementation owners; they create no new runtime lane or Jam gate.\n\n1. P2 — Reconcile the general application surface with declared acts (lines163–170,219–228,335–407,476–479,517–524). The seven acts and claim/note Jam conventions are the legacy/code-review profile, not the platform's permanent vocabulary. Current main e6e67828 protocol33 declares application kinds and signed per-kind bindings; the code-review methods are one application's conveniences. Classify the legacy profile and give declared applications a tools-only route to discover their meanings and submit their actual acts. Reconcile pack mapping and the Jam example with that model, preserving one ordinarily admitted act per write rather than requiring every application verb to be a fixed named code-review call. Keep platform exclusions, current role/delegation authority, frozen historical rendering and retired-kind behavior. Concrete dynamic descriptors/pack installation/evolution remain with the declared-acts/MCP work and N3/N5; do not design a second vocabulary or treat changed annotations/help as authority. The current source contract is not evidence that all runtime stages have landed.\n\n2. P2 — Required keys are necessary but do not preserve prepared meaning by themselves (lines225–228,372–397,519–520). State the exact retry boundary: kind, target, body, binding and signed bytes where applicable remain frozen, including resolved pack revision/mapping/task meaning. A lost reply or process restart must not rerun a changed pack mapping or read a new declaration and sign different intent under the old key. On binding-stale/kind-undeclared, show the changed meaning and let the caller choose a fresh preparation; no automatic re-signing. Name the existing durable owner/caller-retained prepared handle boundary without adding MCP sessions or a public outbox. The example's input/mapping must preserve the required caller key rather than silently lose it. R-IDEM and R-DECL-16 already define this; the unlanded f621 client PreparedAct/replay source illustrates the intended boundary, not completed functional approval. C1/C3/005/007 and existing N4 guidance retain their responsibilities.\n\n3. P2 — Make the result and credential acceptance statements consistent (lines56,197–200,253,517–520). Structured failures are ArtroomError, yet the promised advertised schema includes only success and Refusal. Specify a conforming error branch or a deliberate error-result representation consistent with R-API-1/the selected SDK; do not promise universal conformance while excluding a returned shape. MCP distinguishes protocol faults from isError tool failures and requires advertised structured results to conform. [MCP tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools). Also replace “no token in any result” with the intended boundary: the explicit authorized ready-workspace grant is allowed, while incidental results, attention, logs, errors and caches must not expose it. Retain a useful positive grant outcome and negative disclosure/authority outcome. This corrects the plan; it does not reopen an unchanged approved transport implementation.\n\n4. P2 — Separate OAuth authentication, consent and Room authority (lines186–195,481–482,498,504–524). Redeeming an invitation can identify/join the person; it is not itself consent to an arbitrary OAuth client. Name consent to the client, validated redirect destination, Room resource and scopes, with denial producing no issuance and wrong-room tokens rejected. A valid OAuth token must still map to current Room principal/delegation authority; it grants no owner-control shortcut or token pass-through. Cloudflare's pinned provider distinguishes sign-in from consent, and leaves operation scope policy to the handler. [Consent page](https://github.com/cloudflare/workers-oauth-provider/blob/v1.2.1/docs/consent-page.md), [resource-server responsibilities](https://github.com/cloudflare/workers-oauth-provider/blob/v1.2.1/docs/resource-servers.md). Keep OAuth's full stage2 scope and use existing C1/N3/N6 and MCP owners for its focused implementation/cold-run evidence.\n\n5. P2 — Scope wire caching to the actual cacheable methods (lines106,162,206–209). The target specification requires cache hints for complete discovery/list/resource-read results, not tools/call generally; interim/input-response rounds are not cacheable. Distinguish resource caching from any application cache behind read tools. Keep authenticated Room results private and separated by authorization context; immutable content does not make authority or disclosure permanent, and cacheScope is not access control. [MCP caching](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/utilities/caching). This is a missing planning distinction, not a reproduced cache bypass.\n\n6. P3 — Update dated source status, dependencies and owners (lines42–94,490–545). Preserve the818/697 historical snapshot, then distinguish current main, unapproved composed candidates, deployed evidence and still-owed work. Main e6 contains the MCP Worker adapter source; that alone proves no deployment, while the f621 combined branch remains subject to the original functional reviews/restorations. Do not continue assigning already commissioned outcomes to an ownerless adapter. Reconcile stage1's resources/prompts in497 with adopted item9's stage2 placement in529; preserve the capabilities and record any deliberate sequencing change rather than silently contradicting the decision. Map reads to N1, library/example/onboarding to N3, guidance to N4/N6, pack evolution to N5, and private hosted/prepared meanings to005/007 and C1/C3. Reuse unchanged reviewed evidence; broad stage2 scope is not first-Jam readiness.\n\n7. P3 — Correct and qualify the external compatibility claims (lines98,125–145,313,440–455). On2026-10-03 official versioning identifies2026-07-28 as current, which may receive compatible changes, rather than final. [MCP versioning](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/versioning). Official OpenAI guidance asks that the first512 characters stand alone; it does not establish the note's hard truncation guarantee. Keep512 as Artroom's conservative budget. [Official MCP guidance](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/extend/mcp?surface=cli). Keep historical client/vendor facts dated and qualify release/runtime/configuration claims; the named cold-agent evidence determines support. Retain modern plus legacy stateless operation and polling. Stage3 live subscriptions need a handler/Room routing and recovery boundary: Cloudflare notifications are isolate-local and a per-fetch handler cannot notify an earlier handler's stream. [Cloudflare handler API](https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/model-context-protocol/apis/handler-api/). This keeps the full enhancement scope while avoiding a push-durability assumption.\n\nArchitecture: transport remains separate from Room admission; tools remain complete while resources/prompts/apps improve access; applications retain both declarative packs and their own servers/library. Extend the general declared-act route instead of routing every application through code-review conventions.\n\nSecurity: current authority, prepared meaning, OAuth client consent, private authenticated reads and explicit credential outputs must agree across HTTP, stdio, packs and application servers. A hidden tool, cached descriptor, handle or host click grants no extra Room authority.\n\nSimplification: retain stateless wire handling, ordinary handles and polling, shared descriptors/library and existing operation/input ledgers. Carry the complete schemas/layers, authentication/session behavior, refusals/errors/retries, context budgets, all named harnesses, resources/prompts/library/packs, application servers, cold-agent/tools-only acceptance and later enhancements. No fresh install, runtime/conformance suite, per-field inventory, repeated gate, provider/credential operation or source edit was needed for this planning review. Original implementation approvals and builder's first-Jam judgment stay separate.\n\nValidation: complete frozen note and original request/promise/adopted decision; targeted current protocol and MCP adapter source; exact unlanded prepared-client source; independent full-note primary-document review and root checks of the cited current MCP/Cloudflare/OpenAI boundaries. These are source/design findings, not runtime failure claims.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:91e3d26a57f84b229b09f1fb4e38068415c18d40","binding":"assigned","head":"b5add513f4a6a00c6a4772ef9bcb6f4a6cd6680a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:099e171b0a8778440893b52ff21abe5cf1218f90\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:932ce20e20468fc5503249686dd09151548e818d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:033f64737d0cbcb88c1275234a48615639b02999","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}